{
  "id": "washat_seven_drums",
  "name": "Washat (Seven Drums longhouse songs)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Song worship of the Washat, also called the Seven Drums or Longhouse religion, of Columbia Plateau peoples including the Yakama, Umatilla, Nez Perce, Wanapum and Warm Springs, which took its present form in the nineteenth century under prophets such as the Wanapum leader Smohalla. Services and first-foods feasts in the longhouse are led by seven drummers beating hide hand drums in unison, a leader rings a hand bell to mark the songs, and the congregation sings and dances. The University of Washington Ethnomusicology Archives hold Plateau longhouse recordings. The hand-drum card stands in for the rawhide hand drums.",
  "recipe": "classic modern washat, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. single lead single track no stacking. hand drum source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
